<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Protest and freedom · c. 1914 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="uk">Ой, у лузі червона калина</bdi> / <bdi lang="uk-Latn">Oi u luzi chervona kalyna</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Ukrainian marching song named after the red viburnum, a national symbol. In 2022 people all over the world sang it.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional; three verses by Hryhoriy Trukh</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional; arranged by Mykhailo Kossak (1914)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Ukrainians; the Ukrainian Sich Riflemen</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Marianivka, Kherson Governorate (recorded 1875); Lviv and Stryi, Eastern Galicia (1914)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>rooted in a song of the mid-17th century; recorded 1875; march of 1914</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional words, recorded in 1875, with three verses added by Hryhoriy Trukh in 1914)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Oi u luzi chervona kalyna&quot; (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oi-u-luzi-chervona-kalyna">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</a>&quot;) is a Ukrainian patriotic march. Its text derives from a folk song, &quot;The Steep Banks Overflowed&quot;, which dates from the Khmelnytskyi Uprising of the mid-17th century. The best-known variant was recorded in 1875 by the historians Volodymyr Antonovych and Mykhailo Drahomanov during their ethnographic studies in the village of Marianivka, then in Yelysavethrad county of the Kherson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>. The red viburnum (kalyna) has been part of Ukrainian culture since ancient times: in many ballads its berries stand for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> blood spilled in battle and for untimely death. It is a national symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><p>The song became popular through Stepan Charnetskyi's stage performance Sun of the Ruin, based on a play by Vasyl Pachovsky, which premiered in early 1914 with the melody arranged by the conductor Mykhailo Kossak and was played in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>'s Ruska Besida <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and across Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. In September 1914, after the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Hryhoriy Trukh, an officer of the Ukrainian Sich Riflemen, heard it in Stryi. He wrote three new verses, and it became the anthem of the Riflemen, the first ethnic Ukrainian military formation of the 20th century. According to Ivan Bobersky, its hopeful verses, which named the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> as the Riflemen's aim, set it apart from the mostly sad military songs of the time.</p><p>Between the world wars Alexander Koshetz and Filaret Kolessa arranged it for choir, and it became popular across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> as more than a soldiers' song. Mykhailo Zozuliak, a Metropolitan Opera singer of Ukrainian origin, recorded it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1925, and a second recording, in Koshetz's arrangement, was issued in 1944. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-chaliapin">Feodor Chaliapin</a> also sang it. During and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was used by the Ukrainian Insurgent Army. Because it was tied to the hope of Ukrainian independence, singing it was banned in Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> (1919–1991); it was passed on mostly in families and among dissidents, although a version of its text was printed in a collection of historical songs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1961. In the late 1980s it was sung openly at protest meetings, and the dissident Nadiya Svitlychna wrote a modified version of the text.</p><p>After the Russian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> began on 24 February 2022, Andriy Khlyvnyuk of the band BoomBox, who had cut short a tour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to return and join the armed forces, recorded himself singing the first verse unaccompanied, in army fatigues near Sophia Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>. He posted the video on Instagram on 27 February, and it spread widely. In early March the South African musician The Kiffness made the first remix of it; many others followed. At the end of March the Ukrainian ice dancers Oleksandra Nazarova and Maksym Nikitin performed to the song at the 2022 World Figure Skating Championships in protest at the invasion. In April 2022 Pink Floyd used Khlyvnyuk's voice in &quot;Hey, Hey, Rise Up!&quot;, a single released in aid of Ukrainian humanitarian relief. On 28 June 2022 more than 1,000 singers from over 50 countries sang it together online, which Guinness World Records counted as the largest online performance.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, after the Russian annexation of 2014 and again after the invasion of 2022, singing such &quot;nationalist anthems&quot; was treated as discrediting the Russian army and punished with fines and imprisonment. In September 2022 the Russian occupation authorities jailed and fined members of a wedding party for playing the song, and Sergey Aksyonov, the Russian leader of the peninsula, warned that people singing such songs would be punished harshly.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A marching song of hope and resolve, built around the red viburnum, an old Ukrainian symbol of the blood of fallen fighters. Its soldiers' verses declare that they will fight to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and its mood is confident rather than mournful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Ukrainian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Ukrainian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ой у лузі червона калина похилилася,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Oj u luzi červona kalyna poxylylasja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh, in the meadow the red guelder rose has bent down low,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Чогось наша славна Україна зажурилася.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Čohosʹ naša slavna Ukrajina zažurylasja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For some reason our glorious Ukraine has grown sorrowful.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую червону калину підіймемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju červonu kalynu pidijmemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall raise that red guelder rose up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую червону калину підіймемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju červonu kalynu pidijmemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall raise that red guelder rose up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Не хилися, червона калино, маєш білий цвіт,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ne xylysja, červona kalyno, maješ bilyj cvit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not bend down, red guelder rose, you have white blossom,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Не журися, славна Україно, маєш вільний рід.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ne žurysja, slavna Ukrajino, maješ vilʹnyj rid.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not grieve, glorious Ukraine, you have a free people.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую червону калину підіймемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju červonu kalynu pidijmemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall raise that red guelder rose up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую червону калину підіймемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju červonu kalynu pidijmemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall raise that red guelder rose up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Марширують наші добровольці у кривавий тан,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Maršyrujutʹ naši dobrovolʹci u kryvavyj tan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our volunteers are marching into the bloody dance,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Визволяти братів-українців з московських кайдан.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Vyzvoljaty brativ-ukrajinciv z moskovsʹkyx kajdan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To free our Ukrainian brothers from Muscovite chains.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми наших братів-українців визволимо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našyx brativ-ukrajinciv vyzvolymo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall free our Ukrainian brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми наших братів-українців визволимо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našyx brativ-ukrajinciv vyzvolymo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall free our Ukrainian brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ой у полі ярої пшенички золотистий лан,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Oj u poli jaroji pšenyčky zolotystyj lan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh, in the field, a golden stretch of spring wheat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Розпочали стрільці українські з ворогами тан.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Rozpočaly strilʹci ukrajinsʹki z vorohamy tan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Ukrainian riflemen have begun the dance with the enemies.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую ярую пшеничку ізберемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju jaruju pšenyčku izberemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall gather in that spring wheat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую ярую пшеничку ізберемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju jaruju pšenyčku izberemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall gather in that spring wheat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Як повіє буйнесенький вітер з широких степів,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Jak povije bujnesenʹkyj viter z šyrokyx stepiv,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the wild wind blows from the wide steppes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">То прославить по всій Україні січових стрільців.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">To proslavytʹ po vsij Ukrajini sičovyx strilʹciv.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It will make the Sich Riflemen famous all over Ukraine.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую стрілецькую славу збережемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju strilecʹkuju slavu zberežemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall keep that riflemen's glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми тую стрілецькую славу збережемо,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my tuju strilecʹkuju slavu zberežemo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall keep that riflemen's glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">А ми нашу славну Україну, гей-гей, розвеселимо!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">A my našu slavnu Ukrajinu, hej-hej, rozveselymo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall, hey, hey, make our glorious Ukraine merry!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional, with Hryhoriy Trukh's verses (1914); the Ukrainian text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oi_u_luzi_chervona_kalyna" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ь is written ʹ, х is written x.</li><li>line 1.1: 'калина' is the guelder rose (Viburnum opulus), a bush with red berries and white flowers, a symbol of Ukraine.</li><li>line 2.2: 'рід' is kin, lineage, people.</li><li>line 3.1: 'тан' is a dance; 'the bloody dance' is battle.</li><li>line 5.1: 'буйнесенький' is an endearing intensive of 'буйний', wild, unruly.</li><li>line 5.2: the Sich Riflemen (січові стрільці), the Ukrainian volunteer legion of 1914.</li></ul></details></section>
