<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="iWSMdxAxW3c" data-title="Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow: Thousands singing it in Tallinn in support of Ukraine"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Thousands singing it in Tallinn in support of Ukraine</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Estonia Sings for Ukraine. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iWSMdxAxW3c" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sun of the Ruin (Stepan Charnetskyi, after a play by Vasyl Pachovsky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1914</span><span class="sg-chain-h">stage performance that popularised it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Choral arrangements (Alexander Koshetz; Filaret Kolessa)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement, interwar years</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording (Mykhailo Zozuliak)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1925</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording in Alexander Koshetz's arrangement</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Modified text (Nadiya Svitlychna)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new version of the words, late 1980s</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A cappella video (Andriy Khlyvnyuk)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performance of the first verse, with a changed melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Remix (The Kiffness)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">remix of Khlyvnyuk's recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hey, Hey, Rise Up! (Pink Floyd)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Khlyvnyuk's recording used as the vocal track</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Steep Banks Overflowed</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">mid-17th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">folk song from which the text derives</span></li></ul></section>
